# Copy to .env and adjust. Real environment variables win over this file.
APP_NAME=BespokeCore
APP_URL=http://localhost:3000
# Generate with: bespoke key:generate
APP_KEY=
PORT=3000
# Languages: the default, where missing texts come from, and (optional) the supported ones —
# by default every locale with language files (modules/*/lang/<locale>.ts, lang/<locale>.ts).
# APP_LOCALE=en
# APP_FALLBACK_LOCALE=en
# APP_LOCALES=en,de
# Sessions: database (this app: the sessions table of modules/system, from `bespoke make:session-table`) | redis | file (the
# framework's default, one machine) | memory | cookie (not for apps with login: a sign-out can be undone, see packages/session/README.md)
SESSION_DRIVER=database
# Which database or Redis connection the sessions use (default: default). Never the app's transactions (E108): this app gives
# them — and the cache — a SQLite file of their own, the `system` connection (config/database.ts). With DB_CONNECTION=pgsql
# remove this line: the sessions then open a small pool of their own to the app's database by themselves.
SESSION_CONNECTION=system
# SESSION_LIFETIME=120
# Expired sessions are removed now and then after a response (default 2/100) — not in this app: its scheduler runs
# `bespoke session:prune` (modules/system/schedule.ts), and then requests draw no lottery
# SESSION_LOTTERY=2/100
# In production (HTTPS only):
# SESSION_SECURE_COOKIE=true

# Cache, rate limits (login throttle, live validation) and locks — atomic in every store:
# database (this app: the cache/cache_locks tables of modules/system, from `bespoke make:cache-table`) | redis | file | memory
CACHE_DRIVER=database
# Which database or Redis connection the cache uses (default: default) — like the sessions: the `system` SQLite file, so rate
# limits and locks work inside transactions and survive their rollback (E108). With DB_CONNECTION=pgsql remove this line.
CACHE_CONNECTION=system

# Queue (jobs like the welcome mail; the auth mails go to the `mail` queue — in production: bespoke queue:work --queue=mail,default): database (this app: the jobs/failed_jobs tables of modules/system, from
# `bespoke make:queue-table`; `bespoke dev` starts a worker on every queue, in production run `bespoke queue:work` under a supervisor) |
# redis (lists in Redis — REDIS_URL below; failed jobs still go to failed_jobs) | sync (runs each job when it is dispatched — scripts, debugging)
QUEUE_CONNECTION=database
# QUEUE_CONNECTION=redis
# The database connection of the jobs table, the default queue, and the seconds before a claimed job whose worker died runs
# again — longer than any job's timeout plus the worker's --stop-timeout (queue:work refuses to start otherwise)
# DB_QUEUE_CONNECTION=default
# DB_QUEUE=default
# DB_QUEUE_RETRY_AFTER=90
# The same for QUEUE_CONNECTION=redis: the Redis connection (default: REDIS_URL), the default queue, the seconds of a claim
# REDIS_QUEUE_CONNECTION=default
# REDIS_QUEUE=default
# REDIS_QUEUE_RETRY_AFTER=90
# Failed jobs: database (failed_jobs — bespoke queue:failed / queue:retry) | null (only logged)
# QUEUE_FAILED_DRIVER=database
# Unique jobs, withoutOverlapping and job.once keep their locks and markers in the cache above (CACHE_DRIVER) — with workers on
# several machines a shared one: database or redis.

# Scheduler (modules/*/schedule.ts): `bespoke dev` starts `bespoke schedule:work`; in production run it under a supervisor
# (one per server — tasks with onOneServer() then run once, which needs the shared cache above: database or redis, never
# file or memory) or call `bespoke schedule:run` from cron every minute. `bespoke schedule:list` shows the tasks.
# Whose clocks the tasks' times are on, unless a task says .timezone(…) (default UTC):
# SCHEDULE_TIMEZONE=Europe/Berlin

# Notifications (the bell, `/notifications`): their rows go to the notifications table of modules/system (from
# `bespoke make:notifications-table`) on this database connection — the app's own data, it joins the app's transactions
# NOTIFICATIONS_CONNECTION=default
# Their jobs and mails run on the queue above (the "new sign-in" notice on `mail`); `bespoke notifications:prune` runs nightly.

# Files (config/storage.ts): `local` (private: storage/app/private — files leave only through signed URLs that expire, e.g.
# "Download my data") and `public` (storage/app/public, served under /storage: raster images inline, everything else as a
# download). An S3 disk — AWS or an S3-compatible store — needs the AWS SDK (pnpm add @aws-sdk/client-s3
# @aws-sdk/s3-request-presigner) and these; without them the `s3` disk fails when it is first used. `docker compose up -d`
# in the repo starts one on 56333 (SeaweedFS; its test identity: bespoke / bespoke-secret, create the bucket first).
# S3_BUCKET=bespokecore
# S3_REGION=eu-central-1
# S3-compatible stores only — their endpoint (the bucket then goes into the path):
# S3_ENDPOINT=http://127.0.0.1:56333
# Without these the SDK's own chain is used (AWS_* variables, an instance role, …):
# S3_ACCESS_KEY_ID=
# S3_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=

# Uploads (the avatar in the profile's edit layer): the browser sends a file ahead to the private `uploads` disk (storage/app/uploads),
# the form only a token; on submit the file is checked (size, type by its bytes) and moved where it belongs. Their table
# (temporary_uploads of modules/system, from `bespoke make:uploads-table`) is infrastructure like the cache and sessions (E108):
# the `system` file here — with DB_CONNECTION=pgsql remove this line. `bespoke uploads:prune` clears uploads older than a day.
UPLOADS_CONNECTION=system
# The disk they wait on (private, never the public one), how long a token works and how long the browser has to send the file (minutes)
# UPLOADS_DISK=uploads
# UPLOADS_LIFETIME=120
# UPLOADS_SEND_WITHIN=15

# Live props (W6.10): a page, an island or the bell follows a channel; a change hints every open tab (a Server-Sent-Events stream per
# tab, GET /_bespoke/live) and they reload what changed. redis: every process hears every hint — this app needs it, since the bell's
# hints come from the queue worker (the notification's `live` channel) | memory: one process only (hints from a worker reach no tab)
LIVE_DRIVER=redis
# LIVE_REDIS_CONNECTION=default
# Seconds between heartbeats on an idle stream (keep it under every proxy's idle timeout), the browser's reconnect delay (ms),
# streams per process and channels per stream. Development runs HTTP/1.1: a hidden tab closes its stream (six connections per
# host) and catches up when it comes back; behind an HTTP/2 proxy every tab stays connected.
# LIVE_HEARTBEAT=20
# LIVE_RETRY=3000
# LIVE_MAX_STREAMS=1000
# LIVE_MAX_CHANNELS=20

# Redis (needs the redis package — this app has it): for LIVE_DRIVER=redis, CACHE_DRIVER=redis, SESSION_DRIVER=redis and QUEUE_CONNECTION=redis; `docker compose up -d` in the repo starts one on 56379
REDIS_URL=redis://127.0.0.1:56379
# Prepended to every key — apps sharing one Redis stay apart (default: APP_NAME as a slug, plus ":")
# REDIS_PREFIX=bespokecore:

# Password hashing: argon2id (default) | scrypt — hashes of both are verified either way.
# HASH_DRIVER=argon2id
# Costs (defaults: OWASP) — HASH_ARGON_MEMORY (KiB) / HASH_ARGON_TIME / HASH_ARGON_PARALLELISM,
# HASH_SCRYPT_COST (log2 N) / HASH_SCRYPT_BLOCK_SIZE / HASH_SCRYPT_PARALLELISM

# "Keep me signed in": days (counted from the sign-in) and cookie name; password confirmation ("sudo mode") in seconds
# AUTH_REMEMBER_DAYS=30
# AUTH_REMEMBER_COOKIE=remember_web
# AUTH_PASSWORD_TIMEOUT=900

# Mail: log (default — written to the log, nothing is sent) | array | smtp (needs nodemailer)
# MAIL_MAILER=log
# MAIL_FROM_ADDRESS=hello@example.com
# MAIL_FROM_NAME=BespokeCore
# MAIL_HOST=127.0.0.1
# MAIL_PORT=587
# MAIL_SECURE=false
# MAIL_USERNAME=
# MAIL_PASSWORD=
# Reset links: minutes valid, seconds between two links to one address
# AUTH_RESET_EXPIRE=60
# AUTH_RESET_THROTTLE=60
# API tokens look like bc_12|… — secret scanners find leaked ones by the prefix
# AUTH_TOKEN_PREFIX=bc_

# sqlite (default) | pgsql
DB_CONNECTION=sqlite
# SQLite: the file, relative to the app root. Postgres: the database name.
DB_DATABASE=storage/database.sqlite
# The `system` connection's SQLite file: cache, rate limits, locks and sessions (CACHE_CONNECTION/SESSION_CONNECTION=system)
# SYSTEM_DB_DATABASE=storage/system.sqlite
# Postgres: a URL, or DB_HOST / DB_PORT / DB_USERNAME / DB_PASSWORD
# DB_URL=postgres://bespoke:secret@localhost:55432/bespoke
# Postgres: connections of the app's pool, and of the small pool of the cache and sessions (E108)
# DB_POOL_MAX=10
# DB_INFRA_POOL_MAX=4
# Behind a load balancer: its IPs (comma-separated) or *, so ctx.ip is the client's
# TRUSTED_PROXIES=

# Largest accepted request body (bytes, or 512k / 8m / 1g)
# MAX_BODY_SIZE=8m

# Makes the profile's "Recent activity" slow on purpose (ms), to watch a deferred prop load after the page. Ignored outside development and tests.
# PROFILE_ACTIVITY_DELAY=1500

# Server rendering (default true). false: pages go out without markup and the browser renders them.
# SSR_ENABLED=true
# The SSR lab under /lab (streaming, a page that fails on the server …): on with NODE_ENV=development only; true to measure a production build.
# LAB_PAGES=true
# How long the lab's slow Suspense boundary takes on the server (ms)
# LAB_DELAY=1000

# trace | debug | info | warn | error | fatal | silent
LOG_LEVEL=info
# pretty (the default with NODE_ENV=development only) | json
# LOG_FORMAT=pretty

# Health (E126): /up (liveness) and /up/ready (readiness: database, Redis in use, cache, queue, the private disk). Another path,
# or off when a route of the app needs /up:
# HEALTH_PATH=/up

# OpenTelemetry (E120): spans only when the SDK is started before the app — NODE_OPTIONS='--import ./bootstrap/telemetry.ts'
# (see packages/otel/README.md). The SDK reads its own variables:
# OTEL_SERVICE_NAME=web-app
# OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT=http://localhost:4318
# Request headers recorded on the request span (redacted by name — authorization, cookie):
# OTEL_INSTRUMENTATION_HTTP_CAPTURE_HEADERS_SERVER_REQUEST=accept-language
# OTEL_SDK_DISABLED=true
